// zenohd (router) configuration that makes the ZenSight v1 EVENTS plane durable
// (#583, RFC 09 §2). Events are durable in *transit* (reliable + block QoS) but
// the bus stores nothing, so without a storage there is no history for a late
// joiner: the GUI's startup backfill GET (#536) returns nothing after a restart,
// and a trap that fired while nobody was looking is simply gone.
//
//   zenohd -c configs/router-events-storage.json5
//
// Requires the storage-manager + filesystem-backend plugins (shipped with a
// standard zenohd build): zenoh-plugin-storage-manager, zenoh-backend-fs.
// `just router-plugins` builds a matching pair.
//
// KEYS HERE ARE **FULL**, AND THAT IS CORRECT (#466, RFC 09 §0).
// A *router* has no namespace, so what reaches these storages is the full
// wire key. This example assumes the default base-less deployment (no session
// namespace set — RFC 03 §1.1 amended), where the full key starts at `v1/…`.
// If your deployment sets a base (`zenoh.namespace`, e.g. `zensight`), prefix
// every `key_expr`/`strip_prefix` below with it (`zensight/v1/…`).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY `fs` AND NOT InfluxDB — read this before "fixing" it
//
// RFC 09 §2's sketch puts events in InfluxDB, and its volume table (§2.1)
// reserves `influxdb` for "anything whose value is the *sequence*", marking
// `fs`/`rocksdb` as durable-but-`latest`. That caveat does **not** bind here.
//
// It is a statement about *per-key* history: a `latest` backend loses data only
// when many samples share one key. Every event record owns a unique key — the
// grammar is `v1/<origin>/events/<producer>/<subject...>/<ULID>`, one ULID per
// record, and nothing ever overwrites (RFC 04 §1.3). So "latest per key" and
// "the whole log" are the same set, and `fs` retains everything. RFC 09 chose
// InfluxDB there for uniformity with the `timeseries` storage, not necessity.
//
// What InfluxDB would still buy you is DB-side retention policy — see below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RETENTION IS YOURS
//
// Zenoh storages have no TTL. With `fs`, retention is a disk-space concern:
// prune the volume directory on your own schedule, or size it against your trap
// rate. The registry declares SNMP traps at `burst(1000/h)` with a cardinality
// bound of 100000 per device, which is the number to size against. If you want
// policy-based expiry instead, copy the shape of
// `configs/router-pdns-influxdb-storage.json5` and point this key_expr at an
// InfluxDB volume with a retention policy.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOT `complete`
//
// This storage must never declare `complete`: a complete storage
// short-circuits `BestMatching` and silently collapses a fleet-wide `@rpc` GET
// to a single reply — two sensors answer, the operator sees one, nothing
// errors (RFC 05 §2.1, RFC 09 §2.2). Enforced statically by
// `no_storage_claims_completeness` in `zensight-common/tests/router_storage.rs`,
// which scans every `configs/router-*.json5`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE GUI DOES WITH THIS
//
// `zensight/src/subscription.rs` already GETs `v1/*/events/**` on startup and
// then streams live records through a subscriber; with this storage running,
// that GET is answered by the router. The GUI *also* keeps a local redb cold
// store of its own observations. The two are **additive, not alternative**, and
// need no precedence rule: records are immutable and ULID-identified, and
// `DashboardState::push_snmp_event` rejects a duplicate id and inserts in ULID
// order, so the union is correct however the two arrive.
{
  mode: "router",

  // Listen for sensors + GUIs. Adjust to your network.
  listen: {
    endpoints: ["tcp/0.0.0.0:7447"],
  },

  // NO `timestamping` block, deliberately — unlike
  // `router-evidence-storage.json5`, which needs it because state docs are
  // last-writer-wins mutable keys. An event key is written once and never
  // updated, so "which sample wins" is not a question that has a wrong answer.
  // (A router timestamps by default anyway, so storage replication still works
  // if you later add one.)

  plugins_loading: { enabled: true },

  plugins: {
    storage_manager: {
      volumes: {
        // Persist to disk so the log survives a router restart. Point `dir` at
        // a durable, roomy path; ZENOH_BACKEND_FS_ROOT (env) sets the base the
        // relative `dir` below hangs under.
        fs: {},
      },
      storages: {
        // Every producer's events, every origin. `*` in the origin position
        // never matches a verbatim service origin (D4) — there is no
        // `@catalog` events subject today, so no companion storage is needed;
        // add one explicitly if that ever changes.
        "zensight-events": {
          key_expr: "v1/*/events/**",
          strip_prefix: "v1",
          volume: { id: "fs", dir: "zensight-events" },
          // No `garbage_collection`: nothing ever DELETEs an event record, so
          // there are no tombstones to reap.
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
